            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Synthetic Cubism and Collage</strong><br>\nMoving into Synthetic Cubism, Picasso began introducing textures, patterns, and newspaper clippings into his work, which played with the idea of construction and composition, and led to the invention of collage as an artistic technique.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Classical Period and Surrealism</strong><br>\nIn the 1920s, Picasso's style shifted towards the neoclassical, with a return to drawn form and the human figure. He was also associated with the Surrealists, and his work in this period displayed a blend of his Cubist techniques with Surrealist concepts.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Works and \"Guernica\"</strong><br>\nPicasso's most famous political statement, \"Guernica,\" was a response to the bombing of the Basque town during the Spanish Civil War. This monumental black-and-white mural captured the horrors of war and has become an enduring symbol of anti-war sentiment.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
